{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["Fat of cattle — by the plain sense: a land flowing with milk. And abundant meat for them to eat." "Fat of rams — desert rams fattened in pasture, as written: ירעה מקנך כר נרחב. And likewise: לבשו כרים הצאן — like the end of that verse. And the valleys shall be clothed with grain — the karmim of grain clothed the sheep, full of sheep to graze in them. And likewise: ואויבי ה׳ כיקר כרים — like the withholding of Shaddai of grain crops that are blasted in a single moment near harvest time while it is yet night. They shall vanish in smoke — they shall vanish — in the burning of fire, and their end is that they turn black. And so I myself saw thus at harvest time: so the wicked — first they succeed like the produce of karmim, then night turns upon them and they are crushed." "And rams, the sons of Bashan — the expression 'sons of Bashan' on its own means fat ones, but in other places the karmim fattened in pasture are fat ones from all the herds." "With the fat of kidneys of wheat — the choicest fat of fine wheat here. And like: ותכל דוד המלך. כלתה נפשי — a root of desire and craving. כלה שארי — like from the root שבה שִׁבְיָה, with yod in place of heh in all the segolate nouns of the פעל ה״י verb pattern. And from בנה the nomen actionis is הבניה." "Chemer — and wine of chemer, fully strained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "Rashbam quotes three biblical verses (Psalms 65:14, Psalms 72:16, 1 Samuel 2:10 in order) as support for reading כרים as fattened rams and then for the imagery of the last quote; 1 Samuel 2:10's concluding words are applied to the wicked. The first-person report of observing harvest-time destruction of crops appears to refer to a real experience.", 2 "בייתות ('herds/breeds') is an unusual word; rendered here as 'herds' following context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:39:54.529353582Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}